# HDMI helpers — opt-in drop-ins

cuems-common ships two helper drop-ins for hosts that need them, but
DOES NOT install them in `/etc/systemd/system/` by default. The
operator decides per host.

## When to use which

### `i915-rebind.conf.example`

Forces a PCI rebind of the Intel `i915` driver as `ExecStartPre` of
`cuems-videocomposer.service`. Use on hosts where the HDMI/DP output
goes dark on warm restart due to the i915 TC-port PHY silent-failure
mode (Alder Lake-N + kernel 6.1 VBT 256 mismatch). In the dev lab,
the controller (`000000000002`) needs this; the node clones do not.

### `wake-monitors.conf.example`

Sends DDC/CI power-on commands to known i2c buses 10 s after the
videocomposer starts modeset. Use on hosts whose attached monitors
do not wake reliably from the HDMI signal alone (typically LG
UltraFine 4K when paired with the controller's TC-port path).

> **Controller-only helper.** Both `i915-rebind` and `wake-monitors`
> exist to work around hardware peculiarities of the dev-lab
> controller `000000000002` (Alder Lake-N TC-port DP/HDMI silent
> failure + LG UltraFine that doesn't wake on signal alone).
> **Node hosts do not need either of these** — they boot cleanly
> from the always-on `display-conf-gen.conf` and the videocomposer
> initialises their HDMI outputs normally. The `cuems-wake-monitors.sh`
> shipped here also hardcodes the controller's i2c bus numbers
> (`bus 4`, `bus 11`), which do not even map to the same connectors
> on differently-wired hosts. Do not opt in on a node.
>
> TODO (future refactor): make the script discover bus numbers
> dynamically via `/sys/class/drm/<connector>/ddc/i2c-N` so it
> stops being hardware-specific.

## How to install on a host

```sh
sudo cp /usr/share/cuems/cuems-videocomposer/i915-rebind.conf.example \
        /etc/systemd/system/cuems-videocomposer.service.d/i915-rebind.conf

# (and/or)
sudo cp /usr/share/cuems/cuems-videocomposer/wake-monitors.conf.example \
        /etc/systemd/system/cuems-videocomposer.service.d/wake-monitors.conf

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart cuems-videocomposer.service
```

## How to remove

```sh
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/cuems-videocomposer.service.d/i915-rebind.conf
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/cuems-videocomposer.service.d/wake-monitors.conf
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart cuems-videocomposer.service
```

## Default behaviour (without these drop-ins)

`display-conf-gen.conf` always runs and regenerates
`/run/cuems/display.conf` from `/etc/cuems/default_mappings.xml`. The
videocomposer reads that file at startup. No rebind, no monitor wake.
